==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|targe}}, either:
* from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|targe}}, from {{etyl|frk|en}} {{m|frk|*targa||buckler}};
or
* from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|targa}} (masculine) and {{m|ang|targe}} (feminine);
both ultimately from {{etyl|non|en}} {{m|non|targa||round shield}} from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*targǭ||edge}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*dArg'h-||fenced lot}}. Akin to {{cog|goh|zarga||side wall, rim}} (German {{m|de|Zarge||border, frame}}). However, the soft -g- seems to indicate a French origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/tɑːd͡ʒ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/tɑɹd͡ʒ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A small [[shield]].
#* '''1819''', The Jester wore his usual fantastic habit, but late accidents had led him to adopt a good cutting falchion, instead of his wooden sword, with a '''targe''' to match it — Walter Scott, ''Ivanhoe''

